WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:02.502
[ethereal music]

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:07.440
Why do some worlds have
rings and others don't?

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:15.348
Why no rings for Earth or Mars?

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:18.318
We wouldn't recognize
Saturn without them.

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:21.054
He looks naked
without his rings.

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:24.357
But how did he get them
in the first place?

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:27.594
This is exactly what the French
astronomer douard Roche asked

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:36.536
himself when he looked at Saturn
through his telescope in 1848.

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:40.407
Roche speculated that Saturn's
rings were the debris of a moon

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:43.143
or moons that had
ventured too close

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:45.779
and were pulled apart
by the massive planet.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:49.282
[orchestral music]

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:04.030
Roche was able to
devise an equation

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:06.466
that applies to all worlds.

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:09.502
It tells you how closely a
body can come to a planet

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.438
before it's pulled
apart by the planet's

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:15.108
tidal forces of gravity
and is turned into a ring.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.881
That's the Roche limit.

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:25.051
But until NASA's Cassini
spacecraft executed a series

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:27.587
of daredevil maneuvers
in the Saturn system,

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:29.722
there was a vigorous,
scientific debate

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.758
about when his rings formed.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:34.027
Some astronomers
suggested they were nearly

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:36.329
as old as the planet itself.

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:39.599
More than 4 billion years ago
when the planet coalesced out

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:43.736
of the disk of gas and dust
that surrounded the newborn sun,

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:49.609
a moon or moons likely
violated Saturn's Roche limit.

00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:53.046
Others thought the rings
to be fairly recent,

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:56.816
perhaps only 100
million years old or so,

00:01:56.883 --> 00:02:01.788
and the Cassini spacecraft
proved them right.

00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:03.656
What is Earth's own Roche limit?

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:10.830
If the moon were ever to come
closer than 12,000 miles,

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:12.866
which, by the way,
it's absolutely

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:14.801
in no danger of doing.

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:18.705
[orchestral music]

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:28.414
And it's a good thing
too because I like

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:30.450
our moon right where it is.

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.493
There's only one other
moon in the solar system

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.662
that moves me like ours does.

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:46.032
Maybe it's because it's the
only one with a thick atmosphere

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:48.201
like Earth's and
the kind of surface

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:52.672
features, lakes and mountains,
that remind me of home.

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:58.111
All of this was hidden from view
by a dense layer of orange smog

00:02:58.177 --> 00:03:00.547
until the European Space
Agency collaborated

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:03.683
with NASA to send a
spacecraft to land

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.286
on his mysterious surface.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:11.724
Yes, that would be the one named
after you, Christiaan Huygens,

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:16.496
first to see that world
through your telescope.

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:19.699
[dramatic music]

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:25.505
After an interplanetary
voyage of seven years,

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:29.609
the Cassini-Huygens spacecraft
arrived in the Saturn system,

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:32.946
the fourth of our ships to
venture there but the first

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:36.883
to send a probe to explore
the surface of Saturn's moon,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:44.691
Titan, and to
reveal a moon of far

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:47.827
greater complexity and
splendor than our own rather

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:49.195
dull and lifeless moon.

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:57.203
[soothing music]

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:05.445
As Carl Sagan had predicted
more than two decades before,

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:08.214
there were seas of
methane and ethane,

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:09.749
and there was water ice.

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:16.522
When Cassini first arrived
in 2004 at Saturn's Northern

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:20.259
Hemisphere, it was in
the depths of winter,

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:23.229
and the sun didn't come
out until five years later

00:04:23.296 --> 00:04:27.033
when Saturn's
northern spring began.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.470
Is it just me, or is
this whirling hexagon

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:33.206
at Saturn's north
pole every bit as

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:36.643
exotic as the fantasies
our ancient ancestors had

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:38.011
of these worlds?

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:42.415
The geometrically regular
hexagonal shape of this feature

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:46.152
brings to mind the
handiwork of intelligence,

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:49.055
terraforming,
reworking the surface

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:51.724
for some unknown purpose.

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:54.527
But it's actually the
result of the sudden change

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:56.996
in wind speeds as
vast upwellings

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:59.732
of ammonia rise near the poles.

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:03.102
It's the mother of all
hurricanes, a frenzy

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:05.304
of thunder and
lightning, containing

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:08.441
countless hurricanes within it.

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:12.845
Spring can be a violent,
stormy season on Earth too.

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:16.449
But it was during Saturn's
seven-year-long summer

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:20.219
that Cassini was commanded
to take her own life.

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:24.123
[ethereal music]

